= Knowledge ecosystem — Simple =

A '''knowledge ecosystem''' is a network of people, organizations, tools, institutions, and resources that together create, share, test, preserve, and use [[Knowledge]].

The idea is useful when knowledge does not belong to one organization. A scientific field, for example, can involve universities, laboratories, journals, funders, databases, software projects, professional societies, and companies. No single participant controls the whole system.

== What is in a knowledge ecosystem? ==

A knowledge ecosystem can include:

* people with expertise;
* organizations and communities;
* publications and datasets;
* software and technical standards;
* repositories and search systems;
* funding and incentives;
* rules for review and correction;
* communication and collaboration networks.

These parts affect one another. A new repository can make research easier to find; a funding rule can change what researchers study; a standard can make datasets interoperable.

== More than a document collection ==

A folder full of documents is not automatically a healthy knowledge ecosystem.

Knowledge also depends on:

* whether sources can be traced;
* whether claims are checked;
* whether experts can challenge mistakes;
* whether information is understandable and reusable;
* whether knowledge survives when people leave;
* whether different groups can participate.

A fast-moving network can spread errors just as efficiently as it spreads good information.

== Knowledge moves across boundaries ==

Knowledge ecosystems often cross organizational boundaries.

A university researcher may publish a method, an open-source community may implement it, a company may apply it, and a government agency may create a standard based on it.

This is why the concept differs from traditional [[Knowledge management]], which often focuses on knowledge inside one organization.

== Knowledge ecosystem versus innovation ecosystem ==

A '''knowledge ecosystem''' is mainly organized around producing, searching for, and sharing knowledge.

An '''innovation ecosystem''' is usually organized around creating or delivering innovations through complementary participants.

Real networks can do both. Researchers studying ecosystem types therefore try to distinguish the goal of the system rather than assuming every collaboration is the same kind of ecosystem.<ref name="cobben">[https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jbusres.2021.12.046 Cobben et al. (2022), “Ecosystem types: A systematic review on boundaries and goals.”]</ref>

== Openness and governance ==

Open access can bring more ideas and participants into a system. But openness also increases the need for provenance, moderation, standards, and verification.

Closed systems can protect confidential knowledge, but they can also become isolated.

Governance answers questions such as:

* Who can contribute?
* What evidence is required?
* Who resolves disagreements?
* How are mistakes corrected?
* Who controls important infrastructure?

== Networks are useful but not enough ==

Researchers can map collaboration, citation, funding, or reuse as networks.

A highly connected network may help knowledge move quickly, but connectivity does not prove quality. If everyone repeats the same mistake, the network can be both dense and wrong.

Useful evaluation therefore includes correction, independent verification, reproducibility, and diversity of evidence.

== Artificial systems ==

Search engines, knowledge graphs, language models, and other AI systems can participate in knowledge workflows by finding information, generating candidates, or checking formal rules.

They still need evaluation. A generated answer is not reliable knowledge merely because it is fluent or widely reused.

